// run
// #3167: `vIsIndexable` (compiler/validateexpr.bit) and `checkIndex`
// (compiler/checkmatch.bit) tested a string INDEX receiver's type for
// `TypeKind.Prim && prim == 11` without first defaulting an untyped string
// LITERAL receiver — a literal types as `c.ctx.untypedStringId` (kind
// `UntypedString`, never `Prim`), so `"0123456789"[3]` failed the validator
// with a false E0054 "cannot be indexed" on perfectly legal code (sibling of
// #3165's identical gap in `checkSliceExpr`/`s[i:j]`, this one in
// `checkIndex`/`vIsIndexable`/`s[i]`). Fixing only the validator would have
// exposed `checkIndex`'s own identical gap next: it would then type the
// literal index as `.invalid` instead of `u8`, a silent miscompile one level
// down. A variable receiver (`let d = "..."; d[3]`) was never affected,
// since a named local already carries a concrete type.
fn main() {
  print("${"0123456789"[3]}\n")
}
